<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <LangVersion>9.0</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!--
      ON so that <see cref="..."/> is compiler-checked. This repo carries ~530
      of them and they were ALL unverified text until now: nothing read them, so
      a renamed member left a dangling reference that no build, test or review
      would ever report. Under TreatWarningsAsErrors above, a broken reference is
      now a build failure.

      Measured before flipping rather than after, because the cost of turning
      this on is entirely in what it finds: zero CS1574 (unresolved cref) across
      every project, and four real defects in the mod assembly — two ambiguous
      crefs naming an overload set instead of a member, and one doc comment with
      raw & and > that had been malformed XML for its whole life. All four fixed
      in the commit that set this. A flag whose cleanup cost is unknown is a flag
      you cannot schedule; this one cost three edits.

      The two suppressions below are the entire price, and both are noise rather
      than signal:
        CS1591 - missing XML comment on a public member. 1398 of them, because
                 this project documents what is surprising rather than
                 everything. Enforcing it would be a mandate to write 1398
                 comments nobody asked for, which is how doc comments become
                 ceremonial.
        CS1573 - a parameter without a <param> tag. 18, same reason: the remarks
                 explain the decision, not each argument in turn.
      Neither can hide a wrong reference. CS1574 (unresolved), CS0419 (ambiguous)
      and CS1570 (malformed XML) all stay live, and those are the three that mean
      the prose has drifted from the code.
    -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591;CS1573</NoWarn>

  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Keeps the build machine's home directory out of shipped binaries. Roslyn
    writes the PDB path into each assembly's debug directory, so every DLL and
    the self-contained pbj-peer.exe carried the full absolute path of this repo —
    including into the zips sent to other people. Deleting the .pdb files, as the
    Makefile does, does not remove it: the string lives in the PE, not the pdb.

    Release only, and that is load-bearing rather than tidiness. PathMap rewrites
    the source-document paths in the PDB too, and coverlet resolves coverage
    through exactly those paths — applying it everywhere makes `make test` report
    0% line/branch/method while all 941 tests still pass, which fails the gate in
    the most confusing way available. Everything shipped is built -c Release
    (build, peer, dist, package); coverage runs the default Debug and is
    unaffected.

    PathMap alone is enough: no project sets DebugType, so the SDK default
    portable PDB applies, which is what PathMap needs. DeterministicSourcePaths
    would be redundant and fails the build when SourceRoot inference does not
    kick in.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Release'">
    <PathMap>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)=/_/</PathMap>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
